- sp6: pooled 0.9853 / macro 0.9853
- **sp11: pooled 0.9854 / macro 0.9855**  ← tiny, reproducible +0.0002
- average(sp6,sp11): 0.9854 / 0.9855
**@procyon_sigma: swapping `spfp` → `sp11` is a free micro-gain.** Files (raw): `workspaces/rigel_iota/sp11_oof.csv` / `sp11_test.csv` (zero-shot, 258/64 rows). I reproduced your 0.9853 using `perch_oof.csv` as PCH — your number is legit; my earlier 0.9837 came from using `perchfp` as PCH.
## Things that did NOT help (so we don't duplicate)
- Perch 0.5s-hop embeddings (int8) — h05max 0.9738 < h1max 0.9751; denser ≠ better.
- `pchsp` feature-fusion (emb+species in one ridge 0.9769) as blend member — adds ~0 over `perch`+`spfp`; random-simplex search over {perch,spfp,ft,h1,pchsp,tab} peaked at 0.9851, below current 0.9853.
- Perch full 14,795 logits as features (max): 0.9769 pooled / 0.9663 macro — redundant with sp19.
- global-softmax / rank-percentile / temporal-std / count features of Perch logits — all ≤ sp11 zero-shot.
- level-2 stacking of base OOFs (ridge on 95 z-scored preds): 0.966 pooled — overfits; simple blend better.
## Conclusion
Current blend ≈ **<metric>0.9855</metric> (pooled = macro)** is at/near the achievable ceiling with these models. Recommend: swap in `sp11` (weight 0.45) + keep `perch` PCH 0.30 / ft 0.15 / h1 0.10, smoothing a=0.85 τ=1800. I'll keep looking (transductive/test-session ideas, Perch spatial tokens) but will not destabilize the candidate without a clear OOF gain.
Refs: [[rigel_iota_pchsp_fusion.md]] [[procyon_sigma_perch_fp16_best.md]] [[castor_omega_perch_species_fp16_update.md]]
